#. translators: %d: number of active plugins whose files have not changed in two years.
#: src/Analyze/Rules/AbandonedPluginsRule.php:226
#, php-format
msgid "%d active plugin has not been updated on this server in two years"
msgid_plural "%d active plugins have not been updated on this server in two years"
msgstr[0] ""
msgstr[1] ""

#. translators: 1: plugin name, 2: ISO date.
#: src/Analyze/Rules/AbandonedPluginsRule.php:248
#, php-format
msgid "%1$s (%2$s)"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/AbandonedPluginsRule.php:265
msgid "A plugin with no release in two years is one nobody is watching for security advisories, and one that has not been tested against anything WordPress has shipped since. That is worth knowing. It is not automatically a problem: small plugins that do one thing are sometimes simply finished. This is a reason to go and look, not a recommendation, and Debloater will not deactivate or delete anything."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/AbandonedPluginsRule.php:271
msgid "You did not ask Debloater to check wordpress.org on this scan, so nothing left your server and this reading is the weaker one: when each plugin's files last changed here. That is not a release date. Moving a site by copying files resets it, so a genuinely abandoned plugin can look new; and a plugin whose author has shipped releases you never installed looks abandoned when what is actually stale is your copy. Either way it is worth a look. Run the scan with the wordpress.org check if you want the real release dates."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/AutoDraftsRule.php:84
msgid "Abandoned auto-drafts are still stored"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: number of auto-drafts.
#: src/Analyze/Rules/AutoDraftsRule.php:87
#, php-format
msgid "%s auto-drafts were created and never written."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/AutoDraftsRule.php:90
msgid "WordPress creates an auto-draft the moment somebody clicks \"Add New\", whether or not they type anything. It deletes them itself after a week, but only when its scheduled task runs — on a quiet site that can be a long time. These are rows for posts that were never written."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/AutoDraftsRule.php:95
msgid "Auto-drafts stored"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/AutoloadRule.php:93
msgid "Loaded on every request"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/AutoloadRule.php:94
msgid "Largest autoloaded options"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: amount of autoloaded data, already formatted.
#: src/Analyze/Rules/AutoloadRule.php:99
#, php-format
msgid "%s of options are loaded into memory on every request."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/AutoloadRule.php:103
msgid "WordPress reads every option marked to autoload before it does anything else, on every single request, whether or not that request needs them. Most of it belongs to plugins and is genuinely needed early; some of it is cache timeouts and per-visitor session data that is only read when something asks for it."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/AutoloadRule.php:116
#: src/Analyze/Rules/AutoloadRule.php:133
msgid "A lot is loaded on every request"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/AutoloadRule.php:118
msgid "None of it matches the small set of options Debloater knows to be safe to defer, so there is nothing to propose here — only something to be aware of."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: number of non-shop pages that loaded the script.
#: src/Analyze/Rules/CartFragmentsRule.php:91
#, php-format
msgid "Cart fragments load on %d page that is not part of the shop"
msgid_plural "Cart fragments load on %d pages that are not part of the shop"
msgstr[0] ""
msgstr[1] ""

#. translators: 1: comma-separated page paths, 2: number of pages sampled.
#: src/Analyze/Rules/CartFragmentsRule.php:101
#, php-format
msgid "Loaded on: %1$s. Of %2$d pages sampled."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/CartFragmentsRule.php:105
msgid "The cart-fragments script asks the server what is in the cart every time a page loads, and that request cannot be served from a cache because the answer is different for every visitor. On a shop page it is doing its job. On a blog post it is a round trip for a cart the page never shows. This change keeps the script on every WooCommerce page and drops it elsewhere, deciding page by page as each one is built. If anything on your site shows a cart away from the shop — a total in the header, a widget in a sidebar — Debloater will decline this change instead of offering it."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/CartFragmentsRule.php:110
msgid "Non-shop pages loading cart fragments"
msgstr ""

