#: src/Analyze/Rules/DashiconsFrontendRule.php:106
msgid "The admin icon font loads for visitors"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/DashiconsFrontendRule.php:115
msgid "Dashicons is being loaded on the front end, where logged-out visitors download the whole icon font."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/DashiconsFrontendRule.php:124
msgid "Dashicons is WordPress's admin icon font. Core loads it on the front end only for the admin bar, which logged-out visitors never see — but themes and plugins often enqueue it for a menu toggle or a search icon, and then every visitor downloads a font for two glyphs."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/DashiconsFrontendRule.php:133
msgid "Dashicons on the front end"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/DuplicateFunctionalityRule.php:63
msgid "Two page caches is the case that genuinely breaks: each caches the other's output, and purging one leaves the other still serving what you just purged."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/DuplicateFunctionalityRule.php:67
msgid "Two SEO plugins write the same titles, canonicals and structured data into the same <head>. Which one wins comes down to hook priority rather than to anything you chose."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/DuplicateFunctionalityRule.php:71
msgid "Two security plugins is rarely broken, but it is two sets of firewall rules, two scan schedules, and two places to look when something legitimate gets blocked."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/DuplicateFunctionalityRule.php:75
msgid "Two image optimizers compress each other's output. That loses quality for nothing, and can leave the media library with two competing sets of derivative files."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/DuplicateFunctionalityRule.php:79
msgid "Several forms plugins is often deliberate — one came bundled with a theme, or an old one still serves a single legacy form. Worth knowing, rarely worth acting on."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/DuplicateFunctionalityRule.php:83
msgid "Two backup plugins means two schedules, two destinations, and a restore that has to pick one. Sometimes that is on purpose."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/DuplicateFunctionalityRule.php:87
msgid "Two analytics plugins usually means two tracking scripts on every page, and often two copies of the same one."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: number of jobs being done by more than one active plugin.
#: src/Analyze/Rules/DuplicateFunctionalityRule.php:149
#, php-format
msgid "%d job on this site is being done by more than one plugin"
msgid_plural "%d jobs on this site are being done by more than one plugin"
msgstr[0] ""
msgstr[1] ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/DuplicateFunctionalityRule.php:160
msgid "Active plugins by category"
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: category name, 2: comma-separated plugin slugs.
#: src/Analyze/Rules/DuplicateFunctionalityRule.php:233
#, php-format
msgid "%1$s: %2$s."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/DuplicateFunctionalityRule.php:258
msgid "None of this is automatically a mistake, and Debloater has no way to tell a duplicate from a migration part-way through or an old plugin kept alive for one thing that still needs it. So it says what it sees and leaves the decision with you. Nothing here will deactivate or delete anything."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: number of addon packs, 2: widgets available, 3: widgets seen in use, 4: widgets potentially unused.
