#: src/Analyze/Rules/EmbedsRule.php:104
msgid "The site offers its own posts for embedding on other sites"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/EmbedsRule.php:113
msgid "oEmbed discovery tags are in the page head, and the wp-embed.js script loads with them."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/EmbedsRule.php:122
msgid "This is the half of oEmbed that lets *other* sites embed *your* posts as a live preview card. Embedding other people's content in your own posts is separate and is unaffected. On a site nobody embeds, it is a script and two head tags per page for a feature never used."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/EmbedsRule.php:131
msgid "oEmbed discovery"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/EmojiScriptRule.php:109
msgid "A compatibility script for emoji loads on every page"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/EmojiScriptRule.php:118
msgid "The emoji detection script, its inline styles and a DNS prefetch to s.w.org load on every page of the site."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/EmojiScriptRule.php:127
msgid "The script exists to replace emoji characters with images on browsers that cannot display them. Every browser still receiving updates can display them natively, so on almost every site this is a script, a stylesheet and a third-party DNS lookup spent on a problem that no longer exists."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/EmojiScriptRule.php:136
msgid "Emoji detection script"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/ExpiredTransientsRule.php:92
msgid "Expired transients are sitting in the options table"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: number of expired transients.
#: src/Analyze/Rules/ExpiredTransientsRule.php:95
#, php-format
msgid "%s transients have passed their expiry time and are still stored."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/ExpiredTransientsRule.php:98
msgid "A transient is a cached value with an expiry date. WordPress deletes an expired one the next time something asks for it — which means the ones still here are the ones nothing will ask for again. They stay in the options table until something removes them, taking up space in every backup and every scan of that table."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/ExpiredTransientsRule.php:103
#: build/index.js:11
msgid "Expired transients"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/ExpiredTransientsRule.php:104
msgid "Transients in total"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/FileEditorRule.php:76
msgid "Theme and plugin files can be edited from the dashboard"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/FileEditorRule.php:77
msgid "The built-in file editor is available under Appearance and Plugins."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/FileEditorRule.php:78
msgid "The file editor lets anyone with administrator access rewrite PHP that runs on every request. That is convenient once and dangerous every other day: it turns a stolen administrator password into the ability to run code, and it makes it easy to break the site with a typo and no way back. Adding define( 'DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT', true ); to wp-config.php removes it. Debloater does not edit wp-config.php — a plugin that rewrites the file every request depends on can take a site offline by getting one line wrong."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/FileEditorRule.php:83
msgid "File editor"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/FileEditorRule.php:83
msgid "Available"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/FileEditorRule.php:84
#: src/Analyze/Rules/HeartbeatIntervalRule.php:114
#: src/Analyze/Rules/UpdateNagRule.php:99
msgid "Administrators"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/GeneratorTagRule.php:103
msgid "The exact WordPress version is published on every page"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/GeneratorTagRule.php:112
msgid "Every page and every feed carries a meta tag naming the WordPress version this site runs."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/GeneratorTagRule.php:121
msgid "Knowing the version does not let anyone in, but it does let an automated scanner skip straight to the exploits that work against it. Removing the tag costs nothing and removes the site from that shortlist."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/GeneratorTagRule.php:130
msgid "Generator tag"
msgstr ""

