#: src/Analyze/Rules/SpamCommentsRule.php:89
#, php-format
msgid "%s comments are marked as spam."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/SpamCommentsRule.php:92
msgid "Spam comments stay in the comments table, with their metadata, until something deletes them. Only comments already marked as spam are considered here: anything still awaiting moderation is left exactly where it is, because nobody has judged it yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/SpamCommentsRule.php:97
msgid "Comments marked as spam"
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/StoredRevisionsRule.php:91
msgid "A lot of old post revisions are stored"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: number of revisions.
#: src/Analyze/Rules/StoredRevisionsRule.php:94
#, php-format
msgid "%s revisions are stored across this site."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/StoredRevisionsRule.php:97
msgid "Every time a post is saved, WordPress keeps the previous version as a revision. They live in the posts table alongside the posts themselves, so they are in every backup, every export and every query that scans that table. Deleting the older ones keeps the recent history and removes the rest — but a revision is somebody's earlier draft, so nothing here is deleted without a full copy being taken first."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/TrashRule.php:81
msgid "Content is waiting in the trash"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: number of trashed items.
#: src/Analyze/Rules/TrashRule.php:84
#, php-format
msgid "%s items are in the trash."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/TrashRule.php:87
msgid "Trashed content is still in the posts table, with its metadata and its terms, until something empties the trash. WordPress does that itself after thirty days when its scheduled task runs. Emptying it deletes the content permanently, so only items that have been there a while are touched — something trashed this morning is very often something about to be untrashed."
msgstr ""

#: src/Analyze/Rules/TrashRule.php:92
msgid "Items in the trash"
msgstr ""

